<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iziaslav I of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1024–1078</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Iziaslav Yaroslavich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iziaslav I was grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and the eldest surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, succeeding him in 1054. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> defeated him on the Alta River in 1068 the people of Kiev drove him out; he returned with Polish help, was expelled again by his brothers in 1073 and regained the throne in 1077. He was killed in 1078 fighting his nephews at Nezhatina Niva.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iziaslav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Iziaslav I of Kiev" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Izyaslav_I_with_his_portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Iziaslav was the oldest son of Yaroslav I the Wise by his second wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir and was baptised Demetrius. He succeeded his father after Yaroslav's eldest child Vladimir died before him. In 1043 Yaroslav agreed with King Casimir I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> that Cherven belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and the agreement was sealed by a double marriage: Casimir married Yaroslav's sister Dobronega, and Iziaslav married Casimir's sister Gertrude. From 1045 to 1052 he was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>. He is credited as one of the authors of the Pravda of Yaroslav's Descendants, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a>, and with founding the Kiev Pechersk Monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On Yaroslav's death in 1054 the realm was divided between Iziaslav, Sviatoslav and Vsevolod, who ruled as a triumvirate for twenty years. They defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk">Vseslav of Polotsk</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-nemiga-river">Battle on the Nemiga River</a> on 3 March 1067. In 1068 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> defeated them at the Alta river. A veche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> urged Iziaslav to fight, but he refused even to negotiate, and the Kievans rose against him, freed Vseslav and acclaimed him prince. Iziaslav fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and returned with the help of Boleslaw II, and was received back in Kiev by 2 May 1069.</p><p>By 1073 he had alienated so many people that Sviatoslav and Vsevolod drove him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on 22 March in a seemingly bloodless coup. Boleslaw took his bribes and gave no aid, and in January 1075 he appealed to King Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, while his son Yaropolk went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> for support. Sviatoslav died in 1076, and Iziaslav returned to Kiev on 15 July 1077.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A dispute over the succession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> followed Sviatoslav's death. Iziaslav and Vsevolod drove out Sviatoslav's son Oleg in April 1078. Oleg returned with Cuman support and his cousin Boris and defeated Vsevolod in August. Iziaslav agreed to help and died in battle against Oleg on 3 October 1078, the only incumbent Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to be killed in battle. His children Yaropolk and Sviatopolk ruled the Turov Principality, and Sviatopolk II became grand prince of Kiev.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iziaslav I of Kiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Nizhatyna nyva. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1059" aria-label="Veliky Novgorod on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5214 190 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Iziaslav I of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5664 460h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5664 460h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.52,31.28&amp;year=1059">What was here in 1059? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
